# Commit Philosophy

Commits must reflect:
- logical development progression
- clean engineering practices
- maintainable project history
- professional team standards

The AI must behave like an experienced engineer maintaining a clean repository history.

# Autonomous Commit Behavior

When working on tasks, features, refactors, fixes, or infrastructure:

- Automatically create commits during progress
- Do NOT wait for explicit commit requests
- Do NOT ask for confirmation after every change
- Commit continuously as logical milestones are completed

The AI should think in:
- incremental development steps
- isolated responsibilities
- clean git history

# Commit Grouping Rules

Group commits by:
- feature
- bugfix
- refactor
- infrastructure
- styling
- architecture
- documentation

Avoid:
- giant mixed commits
- unrelated changes together
- monolithic repository history

# Commit Analysis Rules

Before creating commits analyze:
- changed files
- feature boundaries
- architectural impact
- dependency relationships
- logical implementation order

# Conventional Commit Format

Always use:

<type>(<scope>): <summary>

Examples:

feat(auth): add session persistence
fix(chat): prevent duplicated realtime messages
refactor(ui): simplify card layout structure

# Commit Types

Use appropriate commit types:
- feat
- fix
- refactor
- chore
- docs
- style
- perf
- build
- ci

# Summary Rules

Summaries must be:
- concise
- professional
- lowercase
- descriptive

Avoid:
- vague summaries
- generic wording
- unnecessary verbosity

Bad:

update stuff
fix bugs
changes

Good:

feat(profile): add avatar upload support
refactor(home): simplify feed loading flow

# Commit Body Format

Commit bodies must follow:

What:
- concrete technical change
- concrete technical change

Why:
- business motivation
- technical consistency reason
- maintainability improvement

# Commit Frequency

Create commits:
- after meaningful progress
- after stable implementations
- after isolated refactors
- after completing logical milestones

Avoid:
- committing every tiny edit
- extremely large delayed commits
- messy WIP history

# Chronological Consistency

Commits must follow realistic development order.

Rules:
- foundational changes first
- dependent features later
- infrastructure before usage
- refactors before consumers
- UI integration after logic completion

# Timestamp Rules

When generating commit timestamps:
- maintain realistic chronology
- ensure every commit is later than the previous
- use believable development spacing

Preferred spacing:
- 5 to 45 minutes between commits

Preferred ranges:
- 10:00 AM - 12:00 PM
- 1:00 PM - 5:00 PM

Avoid:
- unnatural timestamp jumps
- multiple commits in the same minute
- unrealistic commit bursts

# Git Command Rules

Use actual git commands.

Use:
- git add
- git commit
- git status
- git diff

Never:
- force push automatically
- rewrite history automatically
- squash commits automatically unless requested

# Repository Hygiene

Maintain:
- isolated commits
- readable history
- professional chronology
- predictable progression

The repository history should look like:
- a real senior engineer workflow
- intentional iterative development
- production-quality engineering practices

# Refactor Rules

Refactors should:
- remain isolated when possible
- avoid mixing with features unnecessarily
- preserve clean history boundaries

# Feature Development Rules

Large features should be split into:
- infrastructure
- domain logic
- UI integration
- polishing
- fixes

Instead of one giant commit.

# AI Workflow Expectations

While working:
- think incrementally
- commit incrementally
- preserve history quality
- maintain logical progression

The AI must proactively manage git history professionally.

# Maintainability Standards

A clean git history is part of maintainable software engineering.

Commits should:
- explain project evolution
- simplify debugging
- simplify code review
- improve long-term maintainability

# Final Principle

The repository history should feel:
- intentional
- realistic
- professional
- maintainable
- production-grade